  <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-green-900 mb-2">Companion Planting Guide for Fruit Trees</h1>
  <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mb-6">What to plant near citrus, mango, avocado, fig, stone fruit, apple and pear in Australia, how good the evidence is for each, and which trees need a pollination partner. Updated {{ today }}.</p>

  <div class="prose prose-sm text-gray-700 mb-6 max-w-2xl">
    <p>Companion planting means growing other plants near your fruit trees to help them, by attracting pollinators and predatory insects, building soil, keeping weeds down or holding moisture. Some of it really works, but it's also one of the most myth-laden topics in gardening.</p>
    <p>This guide covers the fruit trees most commonly grown in Australian gardens. Each suggestion is graded by how good the evidence is, and we note the Australian pests and climates that matter. Check the pollinator table before you buy, because getting pollination wrong can cost you years of fruit.</p>
  </div>

